++THE REMEDY AS ACTUALLY PRINTED, under the per-verb contract
++============================================================
++
++Captured from the ticket-lifecycle graph, run 20260821-202959, node
++`ticket.lifecycle.first.launch`, log `launch-refused` — i.e. a real `exec`
++refusal against a real worktree home, not a fixture string.
++
++  ✗   Read it with `<HOME>/bin/cli/skill-manager home drift --home <HOME>`,
++      then clear the gate with
++  ✗   `<HOME>/bin/cli/skill-manager home drift --ack --home <HOME>`
++      (or launch with --ack-drift).
++
++  where <HOME> = …/ticket-lifecycle/proj-TICKET-A/.skill-manager
++
++Three properties, all of them assertions in that node, all passing:
++
++  every_remedy_the_refusal_prints_names_an_existing_executable   passed
++  every_remedy_names_a_resolved_cli_not_a_bare_skill_manager     passed
++  running_the_remedy_opens_the_gate                              passed
++  metric remediesChecked = 2, remedyIsAbsolutelyResolved = 1
++
++READ THE HEAD TOKEN. It is `<HOME>/bin/cli/skill-manager` — a per-home
++executable. Under #229's first shape it was `/usr/bin/env`, which satisfies
++"absolute" and "executable" FOREVER, whatever the resolution behind it does.
++Those two assertions were passing then too, and meant nothing. They mean
++something here.
++
++READ WHERE THE HOME IS. In `--home`, a token no consumer substitutes — not in
++the head token, which `home-sync`'s `remedyArgs` and `close-change.sh`'s
++`run_cli` both drop by design.
++
++AND THE REMEDY WAS RUN. `running_the_remedy_opens_the_gate` executes the
++printed command and requires the gate to open. A remedy that names the right
++home and does not work is still not a remedy.
++
++`ticket.lifecycle.concurrent.close.out` — which parses close-out remedies into
++an argv vector and EXECUTES them, with no shell — also passed, covering the
++class-1 (`home sync --from … --to …`) and class-2 (`unit publish … --home …`)
++spellings the same way.
